\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 County Manager Bill Beasley thanked the staff that are present today. He said this is bittersweet today because this will be the last opportunity he will have in his tenure with Polk County to present this budget. He said the last 12 months continue to present challenging growth dynamics within Polk County and the added uncertainty of potential statewide property tax reform has placed unfortunate strain on an already burdened local government. He said despite such challenges Polk County continues to be a stabilizing factor and a beacon of leadership in providing core services to businesses, citizens, and visitors of Polk County. He expressed his appreciation to the men and women of the Board's staff, constitutional officers, State Court Systems, and the Medical Examiner's office. He said all of the entities have worked cooperative over the past several months to prepare their portions of the proposed budget. He thanked the citizens who have directly and indirectly participated in helping to shape this budget. He said those voices have been heard. He said preparing Polk's cautious and conservative budget, a budget that truly reflects our community's needs and Board directed priorities, is a challenging process under normal circumstances; he said these are anything but normal times. He said even with property tax reform looming this budget addresses the demands by population growth, public safety priorities, regulatory burdens, infrastructure demands, community health challenges, environmental preservation desires, affordable housing pressures, and economic development goals. He said the common denominator in this budget is that it serves the businesses, the citizens, and the visitors of Polk County. He said that focus has not and will not be diminished. He said the proposed fiscal year 2026/2027 budget totals $3,911,064,517. He said this budget maintains the current county-wide general fund millage rates, reflects receipt of and continued community distribution of various federal and state funds associated with Polk's Community Development Block Grant program, Emergency Solutions Grant fund, HOME Investment Partnership funds, remaining portions of the Federal American Rescue Plan investment funds, and the State Housing Investment Partnership funds. He said this budget maintains the Board's commitment to public safety, community health, infrastructure technology, tourism, and community support by adding critical positions and maintaining current priority programs and services. He said the budget maintains the general funds operating reserves at the current levels. He said this budget makes investments in technology upgrades and associated staffing in order to strengthen security protocols, improve operations efficiencies, streamlines work flows, and maximize resource output. He said the budget maintains the Board's commitment to needed capital investments to support public safety and health, public water supplies, transportation capacity, intersection improvements, stormwater management, water quality improvements, habitat restorations, and environmental stewardship. He said the budget addresses quality of life issues related to community health care, behavioral health, and opioid abatement. He discussed the annual cost indexing of select fees for services and special assessments to avoid larger periodic increases. He said subject to the outcome of the property tax reform, this budget will allocate approximately $67 million of one-time funds to address capital project needs. He said the budget continues to invest in the employees by way of a 4% phased salary increase. He said there will be no increase in participating employee programs for the 5th consecutive year. \par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Budget and Management Director Christia Johnson said the total fiscal year 2026/2027 proposed tentative budget is $3.11 billion and the general fund is $764.1 million. She said the property values increased 4.83% countywide and 2.9% in the unincorporated areas; she said that is the lowest property value growth the county has seen since fiscal year 2013/2014. She discussed property growth in previous years. She said there will be no millage increase, solid waste residential assessment indexes at 5%, the fire assessment decreased slightly based on the results of the fire assessment study, and the 6% indexing of the water and wastewater rates will continue. She discussed the one-time project funding and their priority classifications; she reviewed how the funds were allocated between the priorities. She said the priority 2 projects were allocated, but put on hold pending the results of the property tax reform. She said if the reform is passed those funds will be used to replace some of the general fund ad valorem revenue reduction; she said if the reform fails they will bring a CIP amendment before the Board and allocate the funds to those projects. She discussed the comparison of the current year ad valorem budgeted revenue and the proposed fiscal year 2026/2027 proposed budget. She reviewed the total proposed budget and the different fund sources: special revenue funds, general fund, internal service funds, enterprise funds, capital funds, and debt services funds. She discussed the changes since the last proposed budget presentation and said the total change was .01%. She said the general fund makes up 20% of the total budget. She reviewed the general fund operating budget after the transfers and reserves have been removed. She said the total general fund operating budget is $588.3 million. She discussed the allocation of the general fund operating budget's revenues and expenses. She said for the 6th consecutive year they maintained the general fund operating reserves at the full policy level. She said the general fund operating reserves covers about 85% of the total reserves that are within the general fund. She said these funds are used for business continuity. She said this shows good fiscal stewardship and good governance and stability. She said this will also allow for more favorable borrowing rates. She reviewed the fiscal year 2026/2027 through fiscal year 2030/2031 5-year CIP budget. She said the proposed budget contains 54 new positions for support services, infrastructure, public safety and environmental health. She said the county is leveraging technology and it is not growing government unnecessarily. She discussed the current year position count and the growth since fiscal year 2007/2008. She said the population growth has outpaced the employee growth. She said the total fiscal year 2026/2027 proposed tentative budget is $3,911,064,517.\par
\par
Mr. Beasley said the staff see what the citizens see every day; he said the county continues to change. He said this budget represents a substantial investment on the part of the citizens and the businesses of Polk County. He said it is a direct reflection of the demands for services generated by the growing population and the increased costs of goods and services. He said this budget is expanding in direct relation to the various service demands generated by a growing population. He said this reaction to growth has been what the key focus has been over the last four years. He discussed the growth dynamic that drives citizen's and business' expectations regarding the types of services the county provides, the quality of services, and the cost of those services. He said that dynamic will not change should the property tax reform pass. He thanked the Board for providing the needed policy guidance and allowing staff to think creatively to develop this budget. He said the fiscal year 2026/2027 proposed budget is put forth as a reasonably conservative budget intended to address reasonable citizen and business expectations. He said the proposed budget is a formal expression of our plans, goals, and objectives for the upcoming year and is a true reflection of the services that the county promises to the citizens. He said on behalf of the county staff, the constitutional officers and a host of other related agencies, he said it is an honor and a privilege to present the fiscal year 2026/2027 proposed balanced budget. \par
}

