{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Tina Polk Kenemuth said she would like to address items G. 1 - G. 3 seeking approval for close to $45 million for the construction of the Sheriff's office warehousing and facilities project. She asked for the plans to address the living conditions of Animal Control. She said many constituents have come before them pleading for humane and responsible care to no avail. She asked how are they actively working to find solutions to the problems with Animal Control. She said that after the last meeting, Commissioner Troutman told her that the TNVR program will not work in Polk. She said a spay and neuter program could work and is looking at ways to fund that. She stated it should be a part of the county's budget. She bets that there is zero of this for Animal Control improvements. She discussed other items approved at the last meeting. She said there is money for everything and asked what are they waiting on. She said she came here to express her concerns on turning public comments off on social media, Commissioner Scott acknowledged that it is difficult for people to find them and then they all voted to do it. She said it looks and feels like they do not want public engagement. \par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Commissioner Troutman said she would like to discuss Item M. 52. She said this Board has shown their commitment to public safety and to turning Fire Rescue into a destination department. \par
\par
John Hall, President of the Polk County Professional Firefighters, said he is speaking on behalf of more than 600 men and women who serve this county and the families who stand behind them in support. He said it is remarkable what they have accomplished together. He said the impacts will be felt for generations to come. He said since the beginning they have worked a 56 hour work week and back in 2022 they began exploring an opportunity to change that. He said what started as an effort of recruitment and retention became a much bigger conversation about the future of their department. He said last month our firefighters began working a 48-hour work week. He said they have a commitment to transition to a 42-hour work week no later than October of next year. He said this represents the most significant change of the working conditions in more than half a century. He said throughout this process they asked questions, took time to understand the numbers, and made this possible. He said this is a dangerous profession and the dangers do not end when the emergency is over. He said the effects of exposure to carcinogens, physical exhaustion, and repeat exposures to traumatic events follow firefighters long after the shift comes to an end. He said we made it a priority to give firefighters time to rest, recover, and return to work healthy and prepared to serve. He said this is an investment in the health of our firefighters, healthy careers, healthy retirement, and more time at home with family. He said he is proud of what they have accomplished and he thanked them for their leadership, support, and willingness to invest in the future of our firefighters. \par
\par
Commissioner Troutman discussed Item M.47 with CareerSource Polk. She said the board has the names and a commitment of 12 individuals to serve on this board. She said this has been expedient and they have the first meeting set for November 19th and they will have participation from the president of CareerSource Florida. She said the involvement is showing change with Polk Vision and they have already scheduled for our industries to have conversations to begin to understand what businesses and industries are needing to improve the workforce. She said they will work to finish out filling the board requirements.\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
